      "encoding": "utf-8",
      "content": "\\section{Simulation Results}\n\\label{sec:simulations}\n\n\\subsection{Simulation Settings}\n\\label{sec:simulation-settings}\n\nWe consider the satellite uplink in Section~\\ref{sec:system} with the\nparameters in Table~\\ref{tab:simulation-parameters}. Terminal locations\nare sampled uniformly over the beam footprint. The propagation delays,\nDoppler shifts, and array responses are determined by the same satellite\nand terminal geometry. Large-scale propagation effects are compensated.\nThe channel follows the NTN-TDL-D power delay profile \\cite{shen2025}, with the delay\nspread drawn uniformly from $[30,50]$~ns. The line-of-sight component has\na uniformly distributed phase and fixed amplitude, while the diffuse\npath gains are proper complex Gaussian. Channels remain fixed over each\naccess block. Unless otherwise stated, the access configuration uses\n$K=40$, $Q=16$, and $p=0.1$.\n\n\\begin{table}[!t]\n\\caption{Simulation Parameters}\n\\label{tab:simulation-parameters}\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}ll@{}}\n\\toprule\nParameter & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nCarrier frequency $f_c$ & $2$~GHz \\\\\nSatellite altitude & $600$~km \\\\\nBeam radius & $128$~km \\\\\nMinimum elevation angle & $30^\\circ$ \\\\\nUPA dimensions $M_y\\times M_z$ & $4\\times4$ \\\\\nArray element spacing $d_y,d_z$ & $\\lambda/2$ \\\\\nAFDM block length $N$ & $16$ \\\\\nSubcarrier spacing $\\Delta f$ & $30$~kHz \\\\\nChirp parameters $(c_1,c_2)$ & $(7/32,1/320)$ \\\\\nCPP length $N_{\\rm CPP}$ & $336$ \\\\\nResidual-delay support & $[0,699]~\\mu$s \\\\\nDoppler support & $[-41,41]$~kHz \\\\\nPower delay profile & NTN-TDL-D \\\\\nDelay spread & $[30,50]$~ns \\\\\nRician factor & $11.707$~dB \\\\\nDefault $(K,Q,p)$ & $(40,16,0.1)$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Channel Samples and Training}\nThe first-order score network is trained with either\n$N_{\\rm train}=5{,}000$ or $50{,}000$ clean channel tensors of size\n$16\\times16\\times16$. Four candidate channels are sampled from each\nphysical scene before activity masking. The smaller training set is a\nfixed subset of complete scenes from the larger set. Both models use\nthe same architecture and a separate validation set of $5{,}000$\nchannels; training, validation, and test scenes are disjoint.\nAll channels are expressed in the same fixed training coordinates,\nobtained by dividing the physical channel by the fixed reference\nscale $0.2647966453$.\n\nWe train the first-order network from random initialization using\n\\eqref{eq:first-loss}. The noise variance is sampled uniformly on a\nlogarithmic scale over $[10^{-3},10^2]$. Both models use Adam with a\nbatch size of $64$, an initial learning rate of $10^{-4}$, and an\nexponential moving average with decay $0.999$. Training lasts $200$\nepochs, and the checkpoint is selected by the minimum fixed-noise\nvalidation loss. The $5{,}000$- and $50{,}000$-sample runs perform\n$15{,}800$ and $156{,}400$ optimizer updates, respectively. Thus, the\ncomparison uses a common epoch budget and different training costs.\n\n\\subsubsection{Evaluation Metric}\nFor channel estimation, we report the normalized mean-squared error\n(NMSE), computed by pooling error and channel energies over the test set:\n\\begin{equation}\n \\mathrm{NMSE}_{\\rm dB}\n =10\\log_{10}\\frac{\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal T}\n \\norm{\\widehat H_i-H_i}_F^2}\n {\\sum_{i\\in\\mathcal T}\\norm{H_i}_F^2},\n \\label{eq:simulation-nmse}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\mathcal T$ indexes the evaluated channel samples.\nThe denoising experiment uses $500$ independent physical scenes with\nfour channels per scene, giving $2{,}000$ channels at each noise level.\nThe channels within a scene share its geometry. The curves report\npoint estimates from this test set.\n\n\\subsection{Channel Denoising Performance}\n\\label{sec:denoising-performance}\n\nWe first evaluate the conditional-mean estimator\n\\eqref{eq:learned-mean} under the direct AWGN model\n$R=H+W$, with $\\vect(W)\\sim\\CN(\\mathbf0,v\\I_D)$ and known $v$.\nThe nominal denoising SNR is $-10\\log_{10}v$ in the fixed training\ncoordinates, using unit reference channel power. We evaluate\n$\\{-20,-10,0,10,20,30\\}$~dB using the same scene definitions,\ncandidate channels, and standardized noise draws across methods.\nThis experiment evaluates the first-order channel mean independently\nof activity detection and the receiver iterations.\n\nThe reference estimators are least squares (LS), which returns $R$;\nthe Gaussian-prior estimate $P_{\\rm tr}R/(P_{\\rm tr}+v)$, where\n$P_{\\rm tr}$ is the mean element power of the normalized training\nchannels; and a genie-aided conditional MMSE reference\n\\cite{cai2026}. The genie is given the true path geometry, path powers,\nand line-of-sight complex gain. Its curve uses the analytic conditional\nMSE for the remaining Gaussian path gains, normalized by the same\nempirical channel energy.\nWe also evaluate simultaneous orthogonal matching pursuit (SOMP)\n\\cite{TroppGilbertStrauss2006SOMP}, GAMP-SBL\n\\cite{ZhangFanHaoQuan2023GAMPSBL}, GNN-ASPL \\cite{ding2026}, and\noff-grid SBL \\cite{WeiEtAl2022OffgridSBL} by applying their\nchannel-estimation kernels to an identity\nobservation operator. Their receiver configurations and the GNN weights\nare retained, and all methods are supplied with the AWGN variance;\nGAMP-SBL retains its internal noise update. These curves therefore\ncharacterize transfer of the existing implementations to direct denoising.\n\n\\begin{figure}[!t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{denoising.pdf}\n\\caption{Independent channel denoising with known AWGN variance:\n$500$ physical scenes and four channels per scene. The proposed curves\nuse $N_{\\rm train}=5{,}000$ and $50{,}000$; the four sparse-recovery\nbaselines retain their receiver configurations. The genie curve uses\ntrue geometry, path powers, and line-of-sight gain.}\n\\label{fig:denoising-performance}\n\\end{figure}\n\nFigure~\\ref{fig:denoising-performance} shows that the model trained on\n$50{,}000$ channels attains lower NMSE than the model trained on\n$5{,}000$ channels at all six SNR values, with reductions ranging from\n$2.39$ to $7.35$~dB. At $0$~dB, the two NMSE values are $-17.92$\nand $-10.57$~dB, respectively. At $30$~dB, the larger model achieves\n$-40.22$~dB, compared with $-29.93$~dB for LS. The low-SNR case remains\nmore challenging: at $-20$~dB, off-grid SBL achieves $-1.81$~dB,\nwhereas the larger proposed model gives $-0.27$~dB.\n\nThe four transferred baselines exhibit an error floor near $-11$~dB.\nFor SOMP, GAMP-SBL, and GNN-ASPL, the integer delay--Doppler dictionary\nlimits the channel representation. For off-grid SBL, the retained\nimplementation estimates coefficients with a first-order atom\napproximation and reconstructs the channel with exact atoms, introducing\nan additional model discrepancy. Accordingly, the high-SNR gaps in\nFig.~\\ref{fig:denoising-performance} include these implementation and\nrepresentation effects; they do not establish the performance of\nfully adapted versions of the four methods. The proposed estimator\nalso remains above the genie reference throughout the evaluated range.\n",
